{"alias":"con_3377422074ef4aea91bee27e88397449","dates":{},"display_label":"Math-3489-A1","record":{"artifacts":[{"location":"in_repository","media_type":"text/markdown","path":"files/attempt.md","role":"manuscript"},{"location":"in_repository","media_type":"text/markdown","path":"files/disclosures.md","role":"supplementary"}],"attribution":{"contribution_mode":"ai_only","humans":[{"authorship_confirmation":"confirmed","display_name":"gavenren","github_id":206281466,"login_at_event":"gavenren","person_ref":"per_00cb1748051d4b19a76e9bc96782efd4","roles":["prompting","submission"]}],"organisations":[],"submitter":{"authorship_confirmation":"confirmed","display_name":"gavenren","github_id":206281466,"login_at_event":"gavenren","person_ref":"per_00cb1748051d4b19a76e9bc96782efd4","roles":["prompting","submission"]}},"claim":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":["The every-form variant is disproved; the published generic-form question is resolved only in the stated partial ranges."],"scope_statement":"Disproves the catalogue's EVERY-form statement with the nonzero cubic e1∧e2∧e3 in six variables, and proves the exact nonzero universal parameter classification: d=1 or n<=d+2, for positive odd d<=n. Gives the whole decomposable annihilator quotient Hilbert series and a family of nondecomposable full-support counterexamples. The published source explicitly asks about GENERIC forms, so this does not disprove that original question. Supplies credited positive generic cases: all n<=d+4 and cubics through n=9, via an elementary triple-cover complement construction and a seven-block integer determinant certificate. Includes exact executable verification, full coefficient/support data and transparent finite test limits. No novelty or unrestricted generic resolution is claimed.","type":"literature_report"},"contribution_id":"con_3377422074ef4aea91bee27e88397449","declarations":{"ai_disclosure":{"actor":"per_00cb1748051d4b19a76e9bc96782efd4","confirmed":true,"evidence_url":"https://githu